<DocsLayout title="Cloud release" description="How a scmle model is released to a cloud: approval, build and scan, admission gate, digest-pinned deploy, live tests, a signed deployment record, release states, rollback and reconcile. Written down and tested locally; not run on a cloud." current="cloud-release">
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Cloud and release</p>
  <h1>A release is evidence,<br /><em>not a deploy.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">A release is a signed, independently approved model bundle in a pinned image, deployed to staging, tested live, then deployed to production, with a signed record at each step. The pieces are code you can run without an account. The workflows that connect them to a cloud have not been run.</p>

  <div class="diagram">
    <div class="diagram-caption"><span>Evidence chain</span><strong>each step refuses without the one before</strong></div>
    <div class="flow-row">
      <div class="flow-node active"><span>01 / APPROVE</span><b>Signed bundle</b><small>independent review</small></div><i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node"><span>02 / BUILD</span><b>Pinned image</b><small>scan against policy</small></div><i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node"><span>03 / ADMIT</span><b>Gate</b><small>refuses with reasons</small></div><i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node"><span>04 / TEST</span><b>Live checks</b><small>health, schema, inference, security</small></div><i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node active"><span>05 / RECORD</span><b>Signed record</b><small>digest of each exchange</small></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The evidence chain</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Approve the model.</strong> The release registry accepts a version only if its bundle is signed and its promotion approval is an approved, independent, validly signed review of that exact bundle.</li>
    <li><strong>Build and scan.</strong> The cloud-release workflow packages the bundle, resolves the base image to a digest, builds an OCI archive, scans it with Trivy and applies the vulnerability policy. The image digest is the digest in the OCI index.</li>
    <li><strong>Admit.</strong> <code>scmle deploy gate admit MANIFEST --key-file K</code> refuses unless the model is signed and approved, the scan passed for this image, the approver is not the requester and, for production, a signed staging record for the same commit, model and image shows all four live checks passed.</li>
    <li><strong>Deploy by digest.</strong> The workflow pushes the archive preserving digests, refuses if the registry digest differs from the built one, then deploys that digest (ECS: a new task definition and service update; Cloud Run: <code>gcloud run deploy</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Read the platform.</strong> <code>scmle deploy observe</code> turns the platform's own answers into an observation: revision, image digest, readiness, traffic.</li>
    <li><strong>Live tests.</strong> <code>scmle deploy live</code> checks health (<code>/livez</code>, <code>/readyz</code>, <code>/health</code>), schema (<code>/schema</code>), inference (the golden records within the cross-environment tolerance, with the answering <code>artifact_digest</code> equal to the released model) and security (anonymous and forged callers refused, no traceback, a challenge header, HTTPS, and a read-only token that cannot predict).</li>
    <li><strong>Certify.</strong> <code>scmle deploy gate certify</code> repeats the admission checks and additionally requires every live check present, passed and bound to this environment, commit, image and model, and the platform observation to show this image ready. Only then does it sign a deployment record: source commit, model digest, container digest, configuration digest, environment, provider, requester, approver, the digest of each check's exchange and the revision. <code>scmle deploy verify-record</code> checks the signature.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>A refusal prints every reason, exits 2 and writes no record. The service behaviour these checks rely on is on the <a href={`${base}/docs/service/`}>serving contract</a> page.</p>

  <h2>Workflows</h2>
  <p>The cloud-release workflow starts by hand only. Every job checks a guard that passes only if the repository variable <code>SCMLE_CLOUD_RELEASE</code> is <code>true</code>; without it nothing runs. A reusable deploy job handles one environment and declares that environment, so the environment's required reviewers are the independent approval; it is the only place that holds <code>id-token: write</code>. Credentials are the run's OIDC token exchanged for a short-lived role or workload identity. A hosted-controls script fails if a workflow mentions a long-lived key.</p>
  <h3>Setup needed before the first run</h3>
  <p>None of it exists yet:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Environments <code>staging</code> and <code>production</code>, each with required reviewers who may not be the person starting the run, and "prevent self-review".</li>
    <li>The repository variable <code>SCMLE_CLOUD_RELEASE=true</code> and the secret <code>SCMLE_SIGNING_KEY</code>.</li>
    <li>Per environment, variables: for AWS the role ARN, region, ECS cluster and service, and ECR repository; for GCP the provider, service account, project, region, Cloud Run service and repository; for both the service URL. Secrets for the two tokens your identity provider issues for the tests (a predict token and a read-only token).</li>
    <li>The roles and service accounts that the <a href={`${base}/docs/infrastructure/`}>infrastructure</a> creates, applied by an operator with their own credentials.</li>
    <li>A release folder with the registry and the approval for the model. No job fetches a model from a cloud store yet.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Production is deployed with the same image digest staging passed. The workflow does not shift production traffic in steps; the staged allocation below is library code not yet wired into it.</p>

  <h2>Registry bound to the runtime</h2>
  <p>The release registry keeps, beside its registry file, a state file with one state per version and what each environment runs.</p>
  <DocTable label="Release states" head={["State", "Meaning"]} rows={states} />
  <p><code>promote(model, version, plane, environment, image_digest, steps, health)</code> asks a control plane (ECS or Cloud Run) to deploy and to move traffic through <code>steps</code>, and reads the platform back after every request. The registry state, the champion alias (production only) and the ledger change only after the last read shows the revision, its image digest, readiness and the full share.</p>
  <p>A read that disagrees, a platform that does not settle, a health callback that stops the rollout, or a registry that refuses the alias returns <code>done=False</code>, restores the previous revision and leaves the registry files as they were. The refusal is recorded as a <code>deployment</code> row in the ledger. <code>rollback</code> restores the previous known-good revision and changes the registry only after the platform confirms it. ECS rolling updates cannot split traffic, so the ECS plane refuses a staged rollout; Cloud Run can.</p>
  <p><code>deployed_version</code> answers "what does the registry say is deployed" only when <code>reconcile</code> finds no disagreement, so an alias moved around the runtime is never read as deployed.</p>
  <pre><code>{`scmle deploy observe --provider gcp --input service.json --revisions revisions.json --output observed.json
scmle deploy reconcile REGISTRY --model churn --environment production --observation observed.json [--ledger deployment_ledger.jsonl]`}</code></pre>
  <p><code>reconcile</code> exits 1 and lists findings: <code>no_registry_record</code>, <code>revision_mismatch</code>, <code>digest_mismatch</code>, <code>traffic_split</code>, <code>state_mismatch</code>, <code>not_ready</code>, <code>alias_not_deployed</code>, <code>ledger_missing</code> and <code>ledger_disagrees</code>. It repairs nothing.</p>

  <h2>What the tests used</h2>
  <p>The ECS and Cloud Run answers are recorded JSON shaped by the documented <code>DescribeServices</code>, <code>DescribeTasks</code>, <code>DescribeTaskDefinition</code> and Cloud Run Admin API v2 responses, changed by scripted platforms that can lag, never settle or run another image. The service responses behind the live checks are assembled from the serving contract. None was captured from AWS, Google Cloud, API Gateway or a load balancer. <code>DescribeServices</code> carries no image digest, so the adapter reads it from <code>DescribeTasks</code> (or the pinned task definition), which the workflow fetches. That the real services answer as the documentation says, and that the workflows run, is not shown.</p>

  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Not claimed.</strong> A release to a real cloud, a protected environment, federated credentials, a scan of a real image, a live test of a deployed service, registry and live state reconciled on a cloud, multi-region availability, and cost or latency equivalence between providers. Cloud acceptance is not proven.</p></div>

  <p class="meta">Cloud release of the 0.1 beta line</p>
</DocsLayout>
